{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 11, :comments ["<b>Which I command you this day to do them.</b> Our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: 'this day to do them' — and not this day to receive their reward; 'this day to do them' — and not tomorrow to do them. The intention is that this world is the time for deed and not the time for reward, and the World to Come is the time for reward and not the time for deed. This is what Solomon, peace be upon him, hinted, saying (Ecclesiastes 9:10): ״כי אין מעשה וחשבון ודעת וחכמה בשאול אשר אתה הולך שמה״ — that is to say, after death, should one wish to perform a commandment and complete what one lacked here, or to engage in a permitted matter through calculations and other bodily affairs, it is not in one's hand, for on that day one's thoughts perish and all one's reckoning is consumed, and at that time one has no deed or calculation at all, nor knowledge or wisdom to bring forth into actuality. Therefore you should do all the good you are able in this world, while you still have the strength and permission to do it; this is what is said at the beginning of the verse: ״כל אשר תמצא ידך לעשות בכחך עשה״ — before time fails you, do all the good you can while you are yet in your strength. On this account a person is obligated to strive in commandments in this world, which is the world of deed and not the world of recompense, in order to receive one's reward in the World to Come, which is the world of recompense and not the world of deed, as our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: 'One who toiled on the eve of Sabbath will eat on the Sabbath; one who did not toil — from where will he eat?' And one is likewise obligated to be as careful with a light commandment as with a stringent one, for one does not know the measure of a commandment's reward — which commandment has abundant reward and which commandment has little reward. This is what our Rabbis of blessed memory interpreted: 'Be careful with a light commandment as with a stringent one, for you do not know the giving of reward for the commandments.' Therefore one must take care not to weigh the commandments according to what occurs in one's thought, saying, 'I will abandon this commandment on account of that one,' for the ways of the commandments in the knowledge of their reward are hidden and exalted beyond the knowledge of creatures, and our intellect falls short of attaining them, and there is none among us who knows them; thus Solomon, peace be upon him, said (Proverbs 5:6): ״אורח חיים פן תפלס נעו מעגלותיה לא תדע״. And likewise it is fitting for one to delight in them and to perform them with joy, as David, peace be upon him, said (Psalms 119:14): ״בדרך עדותיך ששתי כעל כל הון״, ״בחקותיך אשתעשע לא אשכח דבריך״."], :book :deuteronomy, :voice :rabbeinu-bahya, :lang :en, :chapter 7, :notes {0 "The verse fragment כל אשר תמצא ידך לעשות בכחך עשה is introduced as 'what is said at the beginning of the verse,' apparently referring to Ecclesiastes 9:10 cited just above rather than a separate prooftext; the quotation closely echoes Ecclesiastes 9:10."}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-30T13:46:29.231716516Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}